{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Keel, Mori: \\emph{", "suffix": "} \\cite{K-M}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:a733a37d4b75603e6cb9f5e10c1894f649061d7860adecac897233e51e293e4d", "source_text": "Quotients in Groupoids", "source_text_hash": "sha256:5a1925473cf1367012f1814b46ea7c999909ec783d1d6f2f3f1435927287ea40", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item001"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {"MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0002": "$I_\\mathcal{X} \\to \\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0003": "$\\phi : \\mathcal{X} \\to Y$", "MATH_0004": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0005": "$Y$", "MATH_0006": "$S$", "MATH_0007": "$\\phi : \\mathcal{X} \\to Y$", "MATH_0008": "$\\phi$", "QUOTECLOSE_0001": "''", "QUOTEOPEN_0001": "``"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:f9adb27fdf510e327351bf9ba34528974012126eb764641793994c7b52c398da", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1d367a6c4030d0c6a670f32b59acf528bdad6996c17a6f7bc9cecc3fc0d2e281", "source_text": "It had apparently long been <QUOTEOPEN_0001>folklore<QUOTECLOSE_0001> that separated Deligne-Mumford stacks admitted coarse moduli spaces. A rigorous (although terse) proof of the following theorem is presented here: if <MATH_0001> is an Artin stack locally of finite type over a Noetherian base scheme such that the inertia stack <MATH_0002> is finite, then there exists a coarse moduli space <MATH_0003> with <MATH_0004> separated and <MATH_0005> an algebraic space locally of finite type over <MATH_0006>. The hypothesis that the inertia is finite is precisely the right condition: there exists a coarse moduli space <MATH_0007> with <MATH_0008> separated if and only if the inertia is finite.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:17a0e1b2aa75c87851e502561b25f04233daf0c723037462a98c13ed15bd1094", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item001-quote"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Conrad: \\emph{", "suffix": "} \\cite{conrad}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:47a70bc412d3f80b6a01d213bea58fdb794b911018c6c6411c602d8fa1e255b6", "source_text": "The Keel-Mori Theorem via Stacks", "source_text_hash": "sha256:963ab74b8ddc29b59c34a20bdae99e9e6b0ceaf91e450005608bc54adb47fc98", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item002"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {"CITE_0001": "\\cite{K-M}"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R2", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:f9a3192e60f60ce4f5f9ee7eea489aee711c2e5023f380cb2b4f835e880a1c48", "source_text": "Keel and Mori's paper <CITE_0001> is written in the groupoid language and some find it challenging to grasp. Brian Conrad presents a stack-theoretic version of the proof which is quite transparent although it uses the sophisticated language of stacks. Conrad also removes the Noetherian hypothesis.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:2aca9e9db4afa984d1f2fe8d0012afe16f459d3c5215710be11071684d004b76", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item002-quote"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Rydh: \\emph{", "suffix": "} \\cite{rydh_quotients}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:c88a4d84222c923e68c36995b15a470427132583e822f25a91d18e0895d92471", "source_text": "Existence of quotients by finite groups and coarse moduli spaces", "source_text_hash": "sha256:b265d41e6b73cef7368bee6cdf28c9df178a6772592715f7992f5f9fb078463f", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item003"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {"CITE_0001": "\\cite{K-M}", "CITE_0002": "\\cite{conrad}", "MATH_0001": "$\\mathcal{X}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:0002d42c2adc9172acb55958a77085974084083df107126b40344a331769e8cc", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:dccf72f54fc5416ed0a1f7d32abd05e556b5dbb4ac75019d981b6c8e679e9703", "source_text": "Rydh removes the hypothesis from <CITE_0001> and <CITE_0002> that <MATH_0001> be finitely presented over some base.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:c1ea8c80e41be42cc48486fe007b9307b8e4a57cb8f6f1ca19dfc2eab81c349b", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item003-quote"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item\nAbramovich, Olsson, Vistoli: \\emph{", "suffix": "}\n\\cite{tame}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:9479baa8823ba559cebcb70a623afb981328d93e271a0330931a40e02047c76c", "source_text": "Tame stacks in positive characteristic", "source_text_hash": "sha256:c897baf77cfd000e58f37f36e77b592f21bd21378f3c7415413f269aaf8c9c34", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item004"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {"EMPHCLOSE_0001": "}", "EMPHOPEN_0001": "\\emph{", "LATIN_0001": "\\'etale", "MATH_0001": "$\\phi : \\mathcal{X} \\to Y$", "MATH_0002": "$\\phi_*$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0005": "$\\mathcal{X}$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:0971ad072474f2c34627798e462af96d3916ffe8daf4d0c4a85f48d04736459f", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:1343b2563639c7c7937d7bc6a4656f4f4d0fe9668773ad50ab914671d74ad097", "source_text": "They define a <EMPHOPEN_0001>tame Artin stack<EMPHCLOSE_0001> as an Artin stack with finite inertia such that if <MATH_0001> is the coarse moduli space, <MATH_0002> is exact on quasi-coherent sheaves. They prove that for an Artin stack with finite inertia, the following are equivalent: <MATH_0003> is tame if and only if the stabilizers of <MATH_0004> are linearly reductive if and only if <MATH_0005> is <LATIN_0001> locally on the coarse moduli space a quotient of an affine scheme by a linearly reductive group scheme. For a tame Artin stack, the coarse moduli space is particularly nice. For instance, the coarse moduli space commutes with arbitrary base change while a general coarse moduli space for an Artin stack with finite inertia will only commute with flat base change.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:ba901ed3f68853cacf52be949b1fd66bb57db0a15843d81073ceef84e833b25c", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item004-quote"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "list_item", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {}, "render": {"prefix": "\\item Alper: \\emph{", "suffix": "} \\cite{alper_good}\n\\begin{quote}\n"}, "risk_level": "R1", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:44136fa355b3678a1146ad16f7e8649e94fb4fc21fe77e8310c060f61caaff8a", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:c538bba2e5292ca4827dc7939d85e88be9a37097e35fd7c43d9c229f72fa23c1", "source_text": "Good moduli spaces for Artin stacks", "source_text_hash": "sha256:1fb2b7ddad30b1b0e6eb9ed427f564214b8c5a97a95e8a817f703e3f330b2225", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item005"}
{"chapter": "guide", "node_kind": "quote", "parent_tag": "04UX", "placeholders": {"CITE_0001": "\\cite{tame}", "EMPHCLOSE_0001": "}", "EMPHOPEN_0001": "\\emph{", "MATH_0001": "$[\\mathbf{A}^1 / \\mathbf{G}_m]$", "MATH_0002": "$\\phi : \\mathcal{X} \\to Y$", "MATH_0003": "$\\mathcal{O}_Y \\to \\phi_* \\mathcal{O}_\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0004": "$\\phi_*$", "MATH_0005": "$G$", "MATH_0006": "$X \\subset \\mathbf{P}^n$", "MATH_0007": "$[X^{ss}/G] \\to X//G$", "MATH_0008": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0009": "$\\phi$", "MATH_0010": "$Y$", "MATH_0011": "$\\mathcal{X}$", "MATH_0012": "$\\phi$"}, "render": {"prefix": "", "suffix": "\n\\end{quote}\n\\end{itemize}\n\n"}, "risk_level": "R3", "schema_version": 1, "source_commit": "a04446e57ec1fbc252a871afcec7752fb2807b14", "source_math_hash": "sha256:dfbb15ec3c5f6d524acac001bf97c263f57b915bf9d0fa29b0240a26291ea7da", "source_status": "CURRENT", "source_structure_hash": "sha256:8ba396f8e08bd5ec1438b922c6969ff00d51832323c7075cd76754b4a23f782e", "source_text": "For general Artin stacks with infinite affine stabilizer groups (which are necessarily non-separated), coarse moduli spaces often do not exist. The simplest example is <MATH_0001>. It is defined here that a quasi-compact morphism <MATH_0002> is a <EMPHOPEN_0001>good moduli space<EMPHCLOSE_0001> if <MATH_0003> is an isomorphism and <MATH_0004> is exact on quasi-coherent sheaves. This notion generalizes a tame Artin stack in <CITE_0001> as well as encapsulates Mumford's geometric invariant theory: if <MATH_0005> is a reductive group acting linearly on <MATH_0006>, then the morphism from the quotient stack of the semi-stable locus to the GIT quotient <MATH_0007> is a good moduli space. The notion of a good moduli space has many nice geometric properties: (1) <MATH_0008> is surjective, universally closed, and universally submersive, (2) <MATH_0009> identifies points in <MATH_0010> with points in <MATH_0011> up to closure equivalence, (3) <MATH_0012> is universal for maps to algebraic spaces, (4) good moduli spaces are stable under arbitrary base change, and (5) a vector bundle on an Artin stack descends to the good moduli space if and only if the representations are trivial at closed points.", "source_text_hash": "sha256:cd0d651c54862ab39615e30553be85c70c946082f4a4378678edabfbd5697ec4", "unit_id": "tag:04UX:item005-quote"}
